---
description: Conventions for custom hooks and lib modules
globs: src/{hooks,lib}/**/*.{ts,tsx}
alwaysApply: false
---

# Hooks (`src/hooks/`)

- Filenames use kebab-case: `use-chat.ts`, `use-calendar-selection.ts`.
- Each hook owns one feature's state machine or side-effects.
- Return a named object, not a tuple — makes consumers self-documenting:

  ```ts
  return { messages, draft, setDraft, sendMessage, isSending };
  ```

- Keep side-effects (permissions, camera, calendar, storage, network) in the
  hook — not in the screen that consumes it.
- Wrap handlers in `useCallback` and derived values in `useMemo` when they
  leave the hook (stable references matter for child memoization).

# `src/lib/<feature>/` (pure TS)

- No React imports, no JSX. Must be safely unit-testable.
- Put types next to the feature: `src/lib/chat/types.ts`,
  `src/components/assistant/recent-chat-list.tsx` imports from there — do
  not create a global `src/types.ts`.
- Split files by concern:
  - `api.ts` — network calls + URL constants.
  - `types.ts` — shared types and initial-value constants.
  - Named domain files — e.g. `snellen.ts`, `landolt.ts`, `date-utils.ts`.

# Reference pair

Stateless helpers in [src/lib/calendar/date-utils.ts](src/lib/calendar/date-utils.ts)
drive the stateful [src/hooks/use-calendar-selection.ts](src/hooks/use-calendar-selection.ts),
which in turn powers [src/components/assistant/calendar-picker.tsx](src/components/assistant/calendar-picker.tsx).
Follow the same lib → hook → component direction for new features.
